\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A graph here is a finite undirected multigraph; parallel edges and loops are allowed, and a loop contributes two to its vertex's degree. A bridge is an edge whose deletion increases the number of connected components. A cycle is a connected 2-regular subgraph (including a loop or a pair of parallel edges when applicable). A cycle double cover is a finite list $(C_1,\ldots,C_m)$ of cycles; repetitions in the list are allowed.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every finite undirected graph $G$ with no bridges, does there exist a list of cycles $C_1,\ldots,C_m$ such that
\[
 \sum_{j=1}^{m}\mathbf 1_{\{e\in E(C_j)\}}=2\qquad(e\in E(G))?
\]
For an edgeless graph the empty list is permitted. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.16356v3}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can every edge of a finite bridgeless graph be covered exactly twice by a list of cycles?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 1327 (GRAPH-003). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Sang-il Oum, \emph{A proof of the cycle double cover conjecture by OpenAI: An exposition} (2026), arXiv:2607.16356v3, Section 1, Theorem 1, pp. 1--2. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.16356v3}.
\end{description}
